IMPORT prelude

-- Infectious Diseases Act 1976 (Singapore) -- the duties the Act lays on individuals: notification by doctors and laboratories
-- (s 6), isolation orders (s 15), not spreading a dangerous infectious disease (s 21A and the Second Schedule), the HIV provisions
-- (ss 23 to 25), childhood vaccination (s 46 and the Fourth Schedule), and the general penalty (s 65).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/IDA1976.txt (amendments to
-- Act 13 of 2024, Act 4 of 2025 and S 172/2024 shown in force).
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Cited by 16 of the 527 Singapore Acts deposited in this repository. SCOPED to individual duties. Not encoded:
-- administration, surveillance and premises powers, the public health threat and emergency orders (Part 3A), port health
-- (Part 5), investigation powers, and the prescribed lists and periods (in regulations, not retrieved).
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Infectious Diseases Act 1976 -- duties`

§§ `21A and Second Schedule -- not spreading a dangerous disease`

-- s 21A(1): a person who "knows, or has reason to suspect" that they are a case, carrier or contact of a disease "set out in the
-- Second Schedule" must not expose others to infection in a public place or any place used in common with people outside the
-- household. (2A): nor deliberately or recklessly put a member of their own household at risk. (5): defence -- presence was for
-- medical treatment (at the designated place, (6)) or authorised.
-- Second Schedule: Ebola, MERS-CoV, plague, SARS and yellow fever (item 1 deleted by S 172/2024).
--
-- s 21A is limited to those five diseases. Going to work with measles or influenza is not a s 21A offence.
DECLARE Disease IS ONE OF
    `Ebola`
    `MERS`
    `plague`
    `SARS`
    `yellow fever`
    `measles`
    `influenza`
    `tuberculosis`

GIVEN `the disease` IS A Disease
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the disease is in the Second Schedule` IF
    CONSIDER `the disease`
    WHEN `measles` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `influenza` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `tuberculosis` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the disease` IS A Disease, `knew or had reason to suspect` IS A BOOLEAN, `went to a shared place` IS A BOOLEAN, `was seeking treatment or authorised` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person commits a section 21A offence` IF
        `the disease is in the Second Schedule` `the disease`
    AND `knew or had reason to suspect`
    AND `went to a shared place`
    AND (NOT `was seeking treatment or authorised`)

§§ `15 -- isolation orders`

-- s 15(1), (2): the Director-General may order a case, an at-risk individual, a suspected case (and, for home isolation, someone
-- recently recovered) to be isolated in hospital or at home. (4): an offence to fail without reasonable excuse to go there, to
-- LEAVE "without the permission of the Director-General" (no reasonable-excuse defence on this limb), or to breach a condition
-- without reasonable excuse.
DECLARE Breach IS ONE OF
    `failing to go to the isolation place`
    `leaving the isolation place`
    `breaching an isolation condition`

GIVEN `the breach` IS A Breach, `had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN, `had permission to leave` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the breach is an offence` IF
    CONSIDER `the breach`
    WHEN `leaving the isolation place` THEN NOT `had permission to leave`
    OTHERWISE NOT `had a reasonable excuse`

§§ `23, 24 -- HIV`

-- s 23(1): a person who KNOWS they have HIV must not engage in sexual activity unless they first inform the other person of the
-- risk AND the other person voluntarily accepts it. (1A): unless they have maintained an UNDETECTABLE viral load for the prescribed
-- period ((1B) presumed on prescribed test results and treatment adherence). (5): a person is deemed to know once a positive test
-- result has been communicated to them.
-- (2): a person who does not know, but has REASON TO BELIEVE they have or have been significantly exposed to HIV, must inform and
-- obtain acceptance, OR have tested negative, OR take reasonable precautions. (6): prosecution under (2) needs the Public
-- Prosecutor's written consent.
-- (3): up to $50,000 or 10 years.
-- s 24(1): a person who knows they have HIV must not donate blood or do any act likely to transmit it.
--
-- The person who knows has fewer ways to comply than the person who only suspects: reasonable precautions alone satisfy (2) but not
-- (1); only disclosure and acceptance, or a sustained undetectable viral load, satisfy (1).
DECLARE Encounter HAS
    `knows they have HIV` IS A BOOLEAN
    `has reason to believe they may have HIV` IS A BOOLEAN
    `informed and the partner accepted the risk` IS A BOOLEAN
    `maintained an undetectable viral load` IS A BOOLEAN
    `tested negative beforehand` IS A BOOLEAN
    `took reasonable precautions` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the encounter` IS AN Encounter
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the encounter is a section 23 offence` IF
    IF `the encounter`'s `knows they have HIV`
    THEN     (NOT `the encounter`'s `informed and the partner accepted the risk`)
         AND (NOT `the encounter`'s `maintained an undetectable viral load`)
    ELSE     `the encounter`'s `has reason to believe they may have HIV`
         AND (NOT `the encounter`'s `informed and the partner accepted the risk`)
         AND (NOT `the encounter`'s `tested negative beforehand`)
         AND (NOT `the encounter`'s `took reasonable precautions`)

GIVEN `tested positive` IS A BOOLEAN, `result communicated` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is deemed to know` IF
    `tested positive` AND `result communicated`

§§ `25 -- confidentiality`

-- s 25(1): a person who learns in the course of duties under the Act that someone has HIV or an STD must not disclose identifying
-- information except (a) with consent, (b) for the Act's administration, (c) to the police under the CPC, (d) on court order,
-- (e) to those treating or counselling them, (f) to a blood, organ, semen or breast milk bank, (g) anonymised statistics, (h) to
-- the victim of a sexual assault by the person, (i) to the Controller of Immigration, among others.
DECLARE Recipient IS ONE OF
    `the patient's employer`
    `the patient's treating doctor`
    `a court that ordered it`
    `the victim of a sexual assault by the patient`
    `a journalist`
    `a blood bank that received their blood`

GIVEN `the recipient` IS A Recipient, `patient consented` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `disclosure is permitted` IF
        `patient consented`
    OR  CONSIDER `the recipient`
        WHEN `the patient's employer` THEN FALSE
        WHEN `a journalist` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `6, 46, 65 -- notification, vaccination, penalty`

-- s 6(1): a doctor who "has reason to believe or suspect" a patient has a prescribed infectious disease must notify. (2): so must
-- a diagnostic laboratory.
-- s 46(1): every parent or guardian "must ensure that the child is vaccinated against the diseases set out in the Fourth
-- Schedule" -- DIPHTHERIA and MEASLES. s 49, 50: exemptions and certificates of unfitness.
-- s 65: where no penalty is stated, up to $10,000 or 6 months; on a second or later offence, $20,000 or 12 months.
GIVEN `the disease` IS A Disease
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `childhood vaccination is compulsory` IF
    CONSIDER `the disease`
    WHEN `measles` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `repeat offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the general maximum fine` MEANS
    IF `repeat offence` THEN 20000 ELSE 10000
